This service already has exactly one chokepoint for writes: `db.write()` in `db.js` — every `INSERT`/`UPDATE` in `routes/accounts.js` and `routes/orders.js` goes through it, and it already wraps each call in its own transaction. That's the natural place to hang an audit trail, but I'd implement the actual logging at the database level with triggers, not inside `write()` itself — a trigger fires on every write to a table regardless of what code path produced it (a future route, a migration, a psql session), where app-level logging only fires if every author remembers to call it. For compliance that guarantee matters more than convenience.

The gap: triggers see `OLD`/`NEW` rows and get "when" for free from the transaction timestamp, but they have no idea who's driving the query — `req.user` never reaches Postgres. So the actor has to be passed in-band. Fix `write()` to accept the actor and stamp it on the transaction:

```js
async function write(text, params, actorId) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    await client.query("SELECT set_config('app.user_id', $1, true)", [String(actorId)]);
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } catch (err) {
    await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

(`true` makes it `SET LOCAL`-scoped — cleared at commit, so it can't leak across pooled connections.) Every call site — `accounts.js:8`, `accounts.js:15`, `orders.js:8`, `orders.js:16` — adds `req.user.id` as the third argument, since `sessionUser` (`auth.js`) has already resolved it before any handler runs.

On the DB side, one generic trigger function covers every audited table:

```sql
create table audit_log (
  id          bigserial primary key,
  table_name  text not null,
  row_id      text not null,
  action      text not null,        -- INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE
  actor_id    text,                 -- null = no session context, itself worth flagging
  old_row     jsonb,
  new_row     jsonb,
  changed_at  timestamptz not null default now()
);
revoke update, delete on audit_log from app_role;  -- append-only, even for the app's own DB user

create function audit.log_change() returns trigger as $$
begin
  insert into audit_log (table_name, row_id, action, actor_id, old_row, new_row)
  values (
    tg_table_name,
    coalesce(new.id, old.id)::text,
    tg_op,
    current_setting('app.user_id', true),
    case when tg_op <> 'INSERT' then to_jsonb(old) end,
    case when tg_op <> 'DELETE' then to_jsonb(new) end
  );
  return null;
end;
$$ language plpgsql;

create trigger accounts_audit after insert or update or delete on accounts
  for each row execute function audit.log_change();
create trigger orders_audit after insert or update or delete on orders
  for each row execute function audit.log_change();
```

This also covers the soft-deletes already in the code (`accounts.js:15`'s `deleted_at = now()`, `orders.js:15`'s `status = 'cancelled'`) with no special-casing — they're just `UPDATE`s to the trigger.

The one thing this doesn't give you: request-level context (IP, route, request ID) beyond the actor. If compliance wants that too, log it from the `sessionUser` middleware into a small `request_id`/`actor_id` pair set alongside `app.user_id` via `set_config`, and add that column to `audit_log`.
